<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?><!--
   #82 — camera mode on the app's shortcut list.

   Pixel's Quick Tap ("Open app → <app>", then the gear) and a launcher's
   long-press menu both read the app's SHORTCUTS; without this file the app
   declares none, so the only thing either surface could offer was the ordinary
   entry point (the dictionary screen). This is the declaration that puts the
   viewfinder (ProtoCameraActivity) beside it.

   STATIC, not dynamic. The action is fixed — open the camera — with no state to
   reflect and nothing to compute at runtime, which is exactly what a static
   shortcut is for: it exists from install onward, so Quick Tap works before the
   app has ever been launched, and no code has to push or refresh anything. A
   dynamic shortcut with the same id would be the same declaration in Kotlin plus
   a runtime push, and the two could disagree; dynamic would earn its place only
   if the shortcut grew state (e.g. "last capture"), which is not this ask.

   The intent targets .MainActivity, not the viewfinder:
    - a shortcut's intent is started by the launcher (another app), so it may
      only name an exported component. ProtoCameraActivity is deliberately
      android:exported="false"; exporting it would give every app on the device
      a camera entry point to trigger at will. .MainActivity is already exported
      as the launcher entry, so the shortcut adds NO new exported surface — it
      arrives on a namespaced action MainActivity turns into a camera launch, and
      anything else that can start MainActivity can already do strictly more
      (the dictionary screen). The camera's runtime permission request is the
      same one the pinned button triggers; the action cannot capture anything by
      itself.
    - a shortcut launched straight into the viewfinder would make the viewfinder
      the task's root, and Back out of it would leave an empty task. Through
      MainActivity the dictionary screen is the task root, below the camera.

   The action is a namespaced custom one (CameraShortcut.ACTION_OPEN_CAMERA)
   because a static shortcut's <intent> can carry an ACTION and nothing else —
   there is no extras slot — so the action string IS the signal. MainActivity
   compares it for equality, so the ordinary MAIN launcher tap is untouched.

   No <capability-binding> (Assistant / App Actions): that would need a capability
   verified through Assistant tooling and is not part of this ask. This file is
   the seam where one can be added later without moving the shortcut.
-->
<shortcuts xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <!-- The camera shortcut carries its own mark (#87): `ic_shortcut_camera`, the
         camera iris with the 辞典 dictionary mark, converted from the
         repo-authored source `cam_logo.svg` at the repository root (beside
         `logo.svg`).

         Repo-authored like the viewfinder's `proto_ic_*` chrome, so it adds NO
         row to `app/licenses/components.tsv` — that index is the bundled
         THIRD-PARTY notice index, and the app's own AGPL row covers first-party
         assets. (An earlier note here said a new icon would enter that index;
         that was the wrong index for a repo-authored mark.)

         A static shortcut can only carry a plain drawable, so there is no
         adaptive/monochrome variant here: themed icons are an adaptive app-icon
         feature, and the launcher icon keeps its own `ic_launcher_monochrome`,
         untouched by this issue.

         The short label is the one a launcher must have and must be a string
         resource (the platform rejects a literal); "Camera" matches the control
         on the main screen so the two read as one thing. -->
    <shortcut
        android:shortcutId="camera"
        android:enabled="true"
        android:icon="@drawable/ic_shortcut_camera"
        android:shortcutShortLabel="@string/shortcut_camera_short_label"
        android:shortcutLongLabel="@string/shortcut_camera_long_label">
        <intent
            android:action="com.holopengin.instantjpdict.action.OPEN_CAMERA"
            android:targetPackage="com.holopengin.instantjpdict"
            android:targetClass="com.holopengin.instantjpdict.MainActivity" />
    </shortcut>

</shortcuts>
